course: dictionary
lesson: 6369
title: Mucus: the body's sticky trap
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Mucus is a thick, slimy liquid. Your body makes it inside your nose, throat and other parts to protect them. Read the words around it: «Hairs and sticky mucus in the nose and airways trap dust and germs.» Sticky and trap are the clues. Something that catches dust and germs must be a kind of sticky protection.
explain: Look at the sentence «The nose makes mucus all the time, and most of it is swallowed without you noticing.» Mucus is made all the time, even when you feel fine. Ask yourself: what word would fit here? Slime would fit, so mucus is a slimy liquid.
explain: The word also has a second meaning. Snails and slugs make mucus too, and it is the slime they leave behind as they crawl. The same word works for a body and for an animal, so the context tells you which one is meant.
fact: Mucus means a thick, slimy liquid that protects parts of the body.
fact: «Hairs and sticky mucus in the nose and airways trap dust and germs.»
fact: When you have a cold, you cough up mucus from your airways.
fact: Snot is an informal word for nose mucus and can sound rude, and bogey is a UK word for the same thing.
fact: Mucus is uncountable, so say some mucus, not a mucus.
exercise: In «Hairs and sticky mucus in the nose and airways trap dust and germs», what does the mucus do? | Makes the nose warm | *Traps dust and germs | Lights up the airways | Turns dust into food
exercise: Which animal leaves a mucus trail as it moves? | Cats | Eagles | Horses | *Snails
exercise: What does «cough up mucus» mean? | *Bring thick slime up from the throat and airways | Shout very loudly | Swallow a sweet whole | Sneeze out a feather
test: «The stomach churns food with strong hydrochloric acid, and a layer of mucus stops it digesting itself.» What job does the mucus do? | Adds sugar to the food | *Protects the stomach from its own acid | Makes the food colder | Turns the acid into water
test: «Tiny moving hairs sweep the mucus up so you swallow or cough it away.» What happens to the mucus? | It stays in the nose forever | It is blown straight into the lungs | It turns into dust | *It is swept up and swallowed or coughed away
exam: Which is correct, because mucus is uncountable? | *Some mucus | Mucuses | Two mucuses | A mucus
